### What is the intersection of two linked lists?

Intersection means two singly linked lists literally share nodes from some point onward, forming a Y-shape. After the junction node, both lists follow the same chain. The problem asks for the first shared node by reference identity, not by value. Two nodes with the same value at different memory locations are not an intersection.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StatementNode {
    public String accountId;
    public BigDecimal amount;
    public StatementNode next;

    public StatementNode(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.accountId = accountId;
        this.amount = amount;
    }
}
```

### How does the two-pointer switching method work?

Set `pa = headA` and `pb = headB`. Loop while `pa != pb`. When a pointer reaches null, redirect it to the other list's head. Both pointers traverse exactly `lenA + lenB` nodes, so they align and meet at the intersection.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.StatementNode;

public class IntersectionFinder {
    public static StatementNode findIntersection(StatementNode headA, StatementNode headB) {
        StatementNode pa = headA;
        StatementNode pb = headB;

        while (pa != pb) {
            pa = (pa == null) ? headB : pa.next;
            pb = (pb == null) ? headA : pb.next;
        }

        return pa; // either the intersection node or null
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Shared tail: joint account statements
        StatementNode shared1 = new StatementNode("JOINT", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        StatementNode shared2 = new StatementNode("JOINT", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        shared1.next = shared2;

        // Rajesh's personal chain
        StatementNode rajesh1 = new StatementNode("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("10000"));
        StatementNode rajesh2 = new StatementNode("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("20000"));
        rajesh1.next = rajesh2;
        rajesh2.next = shared1; // merges into shared

        // Priya's personal chain
        StatementNode priya1 = new StatementNode("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("30000"));
        priya1.next = shared1; // merges into shared

        StatementNode intersection = findIntersection(rajesh1, priya1);
        System.out.println("Intersection at: " + intersection.accountId
            + " amount " + intersection.amount);
    }
}
```

```text
Intersection at: JOINT amount 50000
```

### Why does the switching trick work mathematically?

Let list A have length `a + c` and list B have length `b + c`, where `c` is the shared tail. Pointer A travels `a + c + b` before reaching the intersection. Pointer B travels `b + c + a` before reaching the intersection. Since `a + c + b = b + c + a`, they arrive at the same node at the same step.

```text
List A: [R1] -> [R2] -> [JOINT1] -> [JOINT2] -> null
                         ▲
List B: [P1] -> ─────────┘

A travels: a=2 nodes, then c=2 shared, then switches to B: b=1 node = total 5
B travels: b=1 node, then c=2 shared, then switches to A: a=2 nodes = total 5
Both reach JOINT1 at step 3 of their second pass.
```

### How does the length-difference method work?

Compute both list lengths, advance the longer list's pointer by the difference, then walk both in lockstep.

```java
public static StatementNode findIntersectionByLength(
        StatementNode headA, StatementNode headB) {
    int lenA = length(headA);
    int lenB = length(headB);

    StatementNode pa = headA;
    StatementNode pb = headB;

    // Advance the longer list
    if (lenA > lenB) {
        for (int i = 0; i < lenA - lenB; i++) pa = pa.next;
    } else {
        for (int i = 0; i < lenB - lenA; i++) pb = pb.next;
    }

    // Walk in lockstep
    while (pa != pb) {
        pa = pa.next;
        pb = pb.next;
    }
    return pa;
}

private static int length(StatementNode head) {
    int len = 0;
    while (head != null) { len++; head = head.next; }
    return len;
}
```

### Why must the comparison be reference equality?

Two separate nodes can hold the same value without being the same physical node. Intersection is a structural property: the two lists literally share the same node object. In Java, use `pa == pb`, not `pa.amount.equals(pb.amount)`.

```java
// WRONG: value comparison gives false positive
if (pa.amount.equals(pb.amount)) { ... } // different nodes with same value

// CORRECT: reference comparison detects shared node
if (pa == pb) { ... } // same object in memory
```

### What happens when there is no intersection?

If the lists do not share any nodes, both pointers eventually become null at the same step after one full switch. The loop exits with `pa == pb == null`, correctly returning null.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.StatementNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NoIntersectionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StatementNode a = new StatementNode("A1", new BigDecimal("100"));
        a.next = new StatementNode("A2", new BigDecimal("200"));

        StatementNode b = new StatementNode("B1", new BigDecimal("300"));

        StatementNode result = IntersectionFinder.findIntersection(a, b);
        System.out.println("Intersection: " + result); // null
    }
}
```

```text
Intersection: null
```

### What are the edge cases?

Both lists empty, one list empty, identical lists (intersection is the head), and lists of very different lengths.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.StatementNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class IntersectionEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Both empty
        System.out.println("Both empty: " + IntersectionFinder.findIntersection(null, null));

        // Same list (intersection at head)
        StatementNode shared = new StatementNode("S1", new BigDecimal("500"));
        System.out.println("Same list: " + IntersectionFinder.findIntersection(shared, shared).accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Both empty: null
Same list: S1
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The two-pointer switching method is preferred because it needs no explicit length computation and handles all cases uniformly. The reason it terminates even without an intersection is that after one switch each pointer has walked exactly `lenA + lenB` steps, at which point both are null simultaneously.

In production at National Bank of India, compliance needs to find where Rajesh's personal statement chain and Priya's personal statement chain merge into their joint account chain. The intersection finder identifies the exact junction node so compliance can audit the shared portion.

```java
// Production usage: find where two customer chains join
StatementNode junction = IntersectionFinder.findIntersection(
    rajeshStatements, priyaStatements
);
if (junction != null) {
    auditService.flagJointAccountUsage(junction);
}
```
